  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran's 1 June declaration to "completely block" the Strait and suspend mediator-channel communications set the framework for the 8-day gap period. On 2 June Secretary Rubio testified to the Senate that Iran had mined "large segments" of Hormuz and framed reopening the strait as "a predicate for Phase 2" nuclear talks. On 3 June the IRGC claimed retaliatory strikes on vessel "Panaya", US Fifth Fleet headquarters in Bahrain, and an American airbase; CENTCOM confirmed it downed four Iranian drones and struck Iranian radar sites on Qeshm Island. On 4 June Hezbollah formally rejected the Israel-Lebanon ceasefire, demanding full Israeli withdrawal, while IRGC commander Qaani cited that withdrawal as a precondition for any halt to Iran's operations — a closure of the Lebanon track that conditioned the nuclear channel. Iranian negotiator Gharibabadi simultaneously claimed "considerable progress" on draft MOU text. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>On 5–6 June the IRGC claimed to have engaged four tankers attempting Hormuz transit without Persian Gulf Shipping Authority clearance, asserting one was disabled; CENTCOM stated only that it downed four Iranian attack drones and confirmed no commercial vessels were struck — the two accounts conflict, with no TIER1 independent corroboration of a commercial seizure. IMF PortWatch recorded 2 vessels on 7 June, approximately 2% of the pre-crisis daily average of 94 vessels. War-risk premiums remain at 8× pre-crisis levels; six P&amp;I clubs have withdrawn Hormuz coverage; Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, and Hapag-Lloyd suspensions remain in effect. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>On 7–8 June Iran launched its heaviest missile barrage against Israel since the April ceasefire — approximately ten ballistic missiles targeting northern Israel, all intercepted by Israeli air defense with no casualties reported. The IRGC characterized the operation as a "warning" and stated responses would be "broader" if aggressions continued. Trump phoned Netanyahu urging restraint; a mutual operational suspension held as of 8 June. On 9 June Trump publicly stated the deal was in "final throes"; Iran simultaneously rejected the latest US nuclear proposal but — via Omani mediators — submitted a counter-offer, representing continuation of the indirect channel without formal resumption of named-principal negotiations. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>On 10 June the Israeli Ministerial Committee for Legislation approved the Torah Study Basic Law bill, completing a key Haredi concession in an emerging deal between Netanyahu and coalition partners. Reports circulated of an agreement pegging the election date to October 20 — earlier than the statutory October 27 deadline. Smotrich's Religious Zionist Party declared it would not support the bill in its original form but did not depart the coalition. The coalition holds 64 seats as of 9–10 June, above the 61-seat threshold. CENTCOM reported no new strikes on Iranian territory in the prior 24 hours on Day 102; Hormuz traffic registered a 4.7 ships-per-day 7-day average per GlobalSecurity SITREP. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:25%;">Escalation 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:50%;">Protracted 50%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:25%;">De-esc 25%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> If the Omani counter-offer cycle collapses — most likely via a new Israeli strike in Lebanon or Iran exceeding mutual suspension boundaries — N1 resolves NO, F4 resolves NO, and a further military exchange near Hormuz drives war-risk above current 8× levels, locking out shipping recovery through Q3.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> The most probable outcome: N1 and F4 remain in slow-motion negotiation beyond their current horizons, the Hormuz de facto closure persists (F5 NO), mine-clearance does not begin (N2 NO), and both sides maintain the operational suspension without formal agreement — a frozen conflict with 2–5 vessels/day Hormuz traffic.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> If Trump's "final throes" framing materializes into named-principal confirmation of a signed MOU before 23 June (N1 YES), mine-clearance begins within days (N2 likely YES), war-risk premiums fall, P&amp;I clubs reinstate coverage, and major carriers begin phased Hormuz re-entry — but F5's 50/day threshold by 2 July remains structurally tight even on this path.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (Tasnim, IRNA, Fars, PressTV) maintained a consistent dual-track narrative across the 8-day gap: confrontational on military posture, carefully opaque on the negotiating channel. On the military track, Tasnim — affiliated with the IRGC — headlined the 3 June strikes on "Panaya", US Fifth Fleet headquarters, and Qeshm airbase as "retaliation for enemy aggression", framing each IRGC action as strictly defensive. PressTV promoted the PGSA map and the June 5–6 tanker-clearance claims as evidence of Iran's "sovereign authority" over the strait. The rhetoric of a "controlled maritime zone" — active since May — was repeated daily as a fait accompli. IRNA carried Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei's Hajj statement (late May, recirculated): "The region will not return to past conditions; US bases will no longer find safe haven" — a framing that pre-emptively presents any eventual deal as an Iranian achievement rather than a concession. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the diplomacy track, IRNA and Iran MFA reported Gharibabadi's "considerable progress" claim on 4 June in a way that preserved negotiating credibility domestically without conceding to US terms. The FM's "just inches away" formulation — paired with criticism of "maximalist demands" — served the dual purpose of broadcasting willingness to mediators while deflecting domestic hardliner pressure. Tasnim's 9 June characterization of the US proposal as "unacceptable" and Iran's counter-offer as the legitimate framework inverts the blame attribution: Tehran presents the US as the obstacle to agreement. PressTV recirculated Pezeshkian's demand that the US "remove operational obstacles, including the blockade" as a pre-condition — keeping the blockade-lift as a leverage point intact and preserving Iran's ability to exit the Omani channel without domestic cost. The net domestic effect is that the Iranian public is prepared for either a deal presented as victory or a prolonged confrontation presented as resistance. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

